# Orrery — Debug and automation surface

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Orrery carries a debugging and automation surface, `window.__orrery`, so the
game can be driven and inspected from code without moving a real pointer or
waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and
the build installs the finished surface on `window.__orrery` as soon as the
game has initialized. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game
through it, so it is present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a read of the game's state or a pose of one part of it. A
pose sets one thing, and the game's own editor rules, simulation, sigils, and
completion test run from there exactly as they do in play, so a scenario
driven from code behaves exactly like one played by hand.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls
it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Orrery carries a debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven
and inspected from code without moving a real pointer or waiting on real
time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and
the build's `initialize` returns the finished surface beside the state it
built, as the pair `[state, debug]`. The engine returns that same value from
`engine.debug`, and it is reached that way alone: nothing is installed on
the page. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it, so it
is present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a reading or a pose of `OrreryState`, written in the shape
of `update`, because the engine holds the state by value and nothing holds a
writable one. A pose takes the current state, as `DeepReadonly<OrreryState>`,
and returns the next `OrreryState`; a reading takes the state the same way
and returns what it read. A caller drives a pose through the engine's
`apply`, as `engine.apply((s) => debug.spawnMote(s, 0, 0, "dust"))`, and a
reading against the engine's current state, as `debug.snapshot(engine.state)`.
A pose sets one thing, and the game's own editor rules, simulation, sigils,
and completion test run from there exactly as they do in play, so a scenario
driven from code behaves exactly like one played by hand. `src/game.ts`
declares and exports its type, `OrreryDebugApi`.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls
it.
{{else}}
Orrery carries a debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven
and inspected from code without moving a real pointer or waiting on real
time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and
the game instance's `initialize` returns the finished surface. The engine
