## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic: the run advances by
simulated cycles integrated against the delta time the game is given, so an
interval of game time reaches the same state however it was divided into
frames. Everything that interval decides comes out identical whatever the
division: the cycle count, every live pose and grip, the hex every mote
rests on, the filaments, the tallies, the area bank, the fault, and whether
the run completed. The three figures carried as running sums of the frames'
own delta times — `simTime`, `sim.fraction`, and the drawn positions derived
from `sim.fraction` — agree to within the rounding of that sum rather than
bit for bit, and no decision the simulation makes is read off them at that
precision. Two more properties make it driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is
  handed, independent of a canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and
  of wall-clock time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads
  nothing from the renderer. Collision is decided at the sample fractions
  `specs/simulation.md` fixes, never at rendered frames.
- No randomness. The same machine on the same challenge produces the same
  run, cycle for cycle, at any frame rate. The produced effects
  `specs/assets.md` fixes vary from one play to the next, and that variation
  reaches nothing this surface reads.

Audio belongs to the frames. A pose changes the state alone and sounds
nothing, the pointer operations included; the cues a scenario hears come
from the frames advanced after it. The cues of `specs/ui.md` answer the
player's hands and the run's own consequences. The three pointer operations
feed the player's input path, so the edit one of them commits sounds on the
next frame advanced; no other pose raises a cue at all, on that frame or any
later one — a part the machine group places, moves, or removes is silent,
and so is a run `startRun` begins. What a scenario hears after such a pose
is only what the frames it advances produce on their own: a set consuming a
constellation, a fault, a completion.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the runtime layer
beneath the game, and the surface carries no operation for the registered
actions or for the overlay. That layer delivers the actions the game
registers from the key bindings this specification fixes, it reports the
pointer position in the stage's logical units along with the press and
release edges, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `advance` below.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The clock, the keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at
the root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of
them. The engine advances the game frame by frame and can take it off real
time, it drives the actions the game registers, it reports the pointer
position in the stage's logical units along with the press and release
edges, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at
the root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of
them. The engine advances the game frame by frame and can take it off real
time, it drives the actions the game registers, it hands the game the
pointer's ordered samples in the stage's logical units with the press and
release edges, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Orrery's, under Diagnostics below.

The surface's pointer operations do not stand in for the player's pointer.
They feed the same input path it feeds, so a posed press and a player's
press are the same event to the game, and nothing is bypassed: hex, tray,
and tape targeting, drags, lays, and the focus rule all run exactly as they
do for a player.

## The switch and the gates

Completion is the game's one autonomous consequence, and the surface carries
a switch over it. The switch is on when the game is played, `reset` restores
it, `setCompletion` sets it, and the snapshot reports it as `completion`.

| Switch | While it is on | While it is off |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `completion` | A boundary at which every set's tally has reached the challenge's `target` completes the run, records the metrics, marks the challenge solved, and unlocks what its mode unlocks, as `specs/simulation.md` states. | The completion check does not fire. The run carries on past a satisfied target, and nothing is recorded, solved, or unlocked. |

Every other faculty a scenario holds still or exercises belongs to one part,
one gripper, or one mote, and each has its own gate:

| Faculty | Held still by | Exercised by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A part's motion | Leaving its tape cell for the cycle blank, which every part rests on. | Writing an instruction into that cell with `setTapeCell`. |
| A gripper's hold | `releaseGrip`, and leaving `grab` off the tape. | `setGrip`, which takes hold with no `grab` ever running. |
| A part's live pose | Leaving it where `startRun` set it. | `setPoseRotation`, `setPoseLength`, and `setPoseCell`, which move it with no tape running. |
| A wheel's fixtures | `removeMote` on each fixture the scenario leaves out. | Leaving them where `startRun` placed them. |
| The run's clock | `setPaused(true)`. | `setPaused(false)`, and the speed `setSpeed` sets. |
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
| The frame loop | `setAutoStep(false)`. | `setAutoStep(true)`, and `advance` while it is off. |
{{/if}}

A gate changes only what it names. Opening one again resumes that faculty
from the next cycle onward, with no catching up for the cycles it missed.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`ORRERY_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number,
and the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, booleans, and
JSON data. Challenges and solutions use exactly the formats of
`specs/formats.md`, `q` and `r` are axial hex coordinates as
`specs/field.md` counts them, `x` and `y` are in the stage's logical units,
a `part` argument is a part's `id`, and a `mote` argument is a mote's `id`,
both as the snapshot reports them.

Each pose sets one thing and leaves the rest of the game as it stands, so a
caller that wants several things arranged makes several calls, in the order
it wants them. No pose decides an outcome: every motion, collision, fault,
transmutation, delivery, and completion comes from the frames advanced after
the pose.

An argument outside the domain its operation states is invalid, and the call
fails loudly rather than guessing what was meant, except where an operation
states that it normalizes or ignores the call.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation's first parameter is the current state,
`DeepReadonly<OrreryState>`. A pose returns the next `OrreryState`, built
from the one it was handed, which it leaves as it was; a reading returns
what it read. The tables below name the arguments that follow the state, so
`setSpeed(index)` is called as `debug.setSpeed(state, index)`.
{{else}}

Each operation takes only the arguments its row names and acts on the live
game the moment it is called. A pose returns nothing; a reading returns what
it read and changes nothing.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### The clock

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setAutoStep(enabled)` | Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the frame loop advancing the simulation from the wall clock, so the game changes only when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to running itself, which is how a build starts and how it is played. It changes nothing but the switch itself: no screen, no machine, no run, and no cue moves at the call. |
| `advance(seconds, frames)` | Runs `frames` whole frames covering `seconds` of game time, each worth `seconds / frames`, immediately and in order. `frames` is a whole number of at least `1` and defaults to `1`. Each is a real frame, the same update the loop runs followed by a render, so the game's own simulation, sigils, cues, and completion test produce the result and the canvas reflects it. |

Drawing and input are unaffected by the switch either way: the loop keeps
rendering and keeps reading the keys, so a menu still answers a key press
while the simulation is held. The snapshot reports the switch as `autoStep`.

A run advances by `SPEEDS[sim.speed]` cycles per second of game time, so
`advance(1, 1)` and `advance(1, 60)` cover the same cycles and reach the
same outcome, to the precision the deterministic core above fixes; every
cycle runs in full whatever the frame that crossed it. Advancing while the
game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall clock is already
doing.

{{/if}}
### Session

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `reset()` | Restores every declared field of the game's state to its title-screen value: the title screen with its first menu item highlighted, `mode` `campaign`, no challenge open, an empty editor with empty histories, no run, the pointer reported as up, `simTime` `0`, the completion switch on, and all progress cleared, so nothing is solved, every record and stash is empty, only the first campaign challenge is unlocked, and both select screens land on their first row. |
| `snapshot()` | A pure read of the state, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape below. It changes nothing. |
| `setCompletion(enabled)` | Sets the completion switch above. |

`muted` is untouched; the runtime owns muting.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Whether the frame loop advances the simulation is untouched too, so a
`reset` inside a stepped scenario leaves the game off the wall clock.
{{/if}}

A reset leaves the game indistinguishable from a freshly started session, so
anything else the build keeps across frames is derived from what a reset
restores.

### Navigation and progress

`mode` is `"campaign"` or `"extras"` throughout this group, and an `index`
outside that mode's challenge count throws an `Error` naming the bounds.

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setScreen(name)` | Enters the screen `name`, one of `title`, `howto`, `select`, and `editor`, exactly as the real transition into it enters it, as the table below states. |
| `setMode(mode)` | Sets the course the select and editor screens serve. |
| `setMenuIndex(n)` | Sets the highlighted item of the menu the current screen shows, from `0` and below that menu's entry count. |
| `setSelectIndex(n)` | Sets the highlighted row of the current mode's select screen, from `0`. |
| `setHowtoPage(n)` | Sets the how-to's current page, `0` to `HOWTO_PAGES - 1` (`4`). |
| `setUnlockedCount(n)` | Sets how many campaign challenges are open, `1` to the shipped course length. The Extras lock nothing, as `specs/modes/extras.md` states. |
| `setSolved(mode, index, solved)` | Adds `index` to that mode's solved set when `solved` is `true`, and removes it when `false`. The set stays ascending and free of duplicates. |
| `setRecord(mode, index, metric, value)` | Sets one record of one challenge. `metric` is `"cost"`, `"cycles"`, or `"area"`, and `value` is a whole number of at least `0`. A challenge with no record yet gains one whose other two metrics are `0`. |
| `setLast(mode, index)` | Sets the row that mode's select screen lands on. |

A call to `setScreen` that leaves the editor leaves it exactly as leaving it
in play does: a live run is stopped, the open challenge's machine is stashed
as `specs/editor.md` states, and the challenge is closed, so `challenge` and
`sim` both report `null` afterwards. Each name enters its screen:

| `name` | What the call does |
| --- | --- |
| `title` | Shows the title menu, `menuIndex` at `0`. |
| `howto` | Shows the how-to, `howtoPage` at `0`. |
| `select` | Shows the current mode's select screen, `selectIndex` at that mode's `last`. |
| `editor` | Shows the editor over the open challenge, leaving the machine, both histories, and any live run as they stand. With no challenge open it throws. |

No cue sounds at the call, as the deterministic core above states.

### The challenge

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `openChallenge(mode, index)` | The open challenge becomes that mode's shipped challenge at `index`, and the game moves to the editor with an empty machine, empty histories, no run, and the tray derived from the challenge. The snapshot reports it under that `mode` and `index`. A locked row opens like any other. |
| `loadChallenge(challenge)` | The open challenge becomes `challenge`, a challenge document in the format of `specs/formats.md`, leaving the same editor state `openChallenge` leaves. The snapshot reports its `source` as `"custom"`. A document that is not well formed throws an `Error` naming what is wrong with it and changes nothing. |
| `referenceSolution(mode, index)` | A pure read: the build's own reference solution for that challenge, as a solution document, whatever is unlocked or solved. `specs/modes/campaign.md` and `specs/modes/extras.md` require one per challenge; this is where they are reachable. |

A challenge posed by either operation is a challenge like any other, and
every rule of `specs/editor.md` and `specs/simulation.md` applies to it
unchanged. Neither operation touches progress: the unlocked count, the
solved sets, the records, the per-challenge stashes, and both `last` figures
stand as they are. Completing a challenge whose source is `"custom"` touches
no progress and no record.

### The machine

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `clearMachine()` | Removes every placed part, empties both histories, and clears the selection, the cursor, and any live drag. The open challenge and any live run stand. |
| `placePart(kind, q, r, rotation)` | Places one part of `kind`, an arm, wheel, or sigil kind of `PARTS` in `specs/parts.md`, anchored on `(q, r)` at `rotation` `0` to `5`, at length `ARM_MIN_LEN` (`1`) with an empty tape. A `kind` of `track`, `rise`, or `set` throws; each has its own operation. |
| `placeRise(index, q, r, rotation)` | Places the rise for the open challenge's reagent `index`, anchored and rotated the same way. |
| `placeSet(index, q, r, rotation)` | Places the set for the open challenge's product `index`, anchored and rotated the same way. |
| `placeTrack(q, r)` | Places a one-cell open track on `(q, r)`. |
| `extendTrack(part, q, r)` | Appends `(q, r)` to that track's path at its `last` end. |
| `closeTrack(part)` | Joins that track's last cell to its first, exactly as closing a path in `specs/editor.md` does. |
| `removePart(part)` | Removes one placed part, discarding its tape and its tape-panel row exactly as `part-delete` does, and clearing a selection or cursor the removal invalidates. |
| `setPartRotation(part, rotation)` | Sets that part's rest rotation, `0` to `5`. |
| `setPartLength(part, length)` | Sets that part's rest length, `ARM_MIN_LEN` (`1`) to `ARM_MAX_LEN` (`3`). A part that carries no length of its own — a wheel, a track, a sigil, a rise, or a set, each of which stands at `1` — throws an `Error` naming its kind. |
| `movePart(part, q, r)` | Translates the whole part, a track's path included, so its anchor is `(q, r)`. |
| `setTapeCell(part, col, instruction)` | Writes `instruction`, a name from `INSTRUCTIONS` in `specs/instructions.md` or `null` for a blank, at column `col` of that part's tape, a whole number of at least `0`. Cells between the tape's end and `col` become blanks, and the trimming rule of `specs/formats.md` is applied afterwards. |
| `loadSolution(solution)` | Replaces the open challenge's machine with `solution`, a solution document in the format of `specs/formats.md`, which is exactly the placements above applied in the order `parts` lists them. It empties both histories and clears the selection, the cursor, and any live drag. |
| `readSolution()` | A pure read: the current machine as a solution document, exactly what `loadSolution` would accept to rebuild it. |

Five rules hold across the whole group:

- Each throws an `Error` with no challenge open.
- Each placement is checked against the placement rules of `specs/parts.md`
  alone, and throws an `Error` naming the first rule it breaks. The
  challenge's `permitted` list is a tray rule of `specs/editor.md` rather
  than a placement rule, so none of these reads it.
- None pushes an undo entry, so `undoDepth` and `redoDepth` move under edits
  made through the pointer and the keys alone.
- A new part's `id` is the `id` of the last entry of `editor.parts` in the
  next snapshot.
- While a run is live, a part one of them adds enters the run at its rest
  pose holding nothing, with a wheel's six fixtures on its spoke hexes, and
  a part one of them removes takes its live pose, its grips, and its
  fixtures off the field with it. Nothing else goes with it: a mote the
  part merely carried is left unheld, resting on the hex it stood on,
  exactly as `releaseGrip` leaves one. A part's rest pose and its live pose
  are separate, so `setPartRotation`, `setPartLength`, and `movePart` leave
  `sim.poses` as it stands.

### The editor's hands

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setSelected(part)` | Selects that part. `null` clears the selection. |
| `setFocus(where)` | Sets the focus to `"field"` or `"tape"`. |
| `setCursor(part, col)` | Points the tape cursor at column `col` of that part's row, a whole number of at least `0`. A `part` of `null` clears the cursor, and a part with no tape row throws. |
| `pointerDown(x, y)`, `pointerMove(x, y)`, `pointerUp()` | Report a press, a move, and a release at a logical stage position, feeding the same input path the player's pointer feeds: targeting, selection, drags, lays, and the focus rule all run as `specs/editor.md` and `specs/controls.md` state. |

**Each of the three pointer operations takes effect immediately, when it is
called, rather than being sampled once per frame.** The press, the move, or
the release is resolved before the call returns rather than deferred to the
next frame.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
No frame need pass between them, so a whole machine can be placed from code
without advancing the game at all.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Each returns the state that press, move, or release left behind, and the
next call is handed that state, so a whole machine can be placed from code
without a frame passing between the calls.
{{else}}
The resolution runs through the same per-sample path the player controller
feeds each frame. No frame need pass between them, so a whole machine can be
placed from code without advancing the game at all.
{{/if}}

The three sound nothing at the call, as every pose does. The cue of an edit
one of them commits sounds on the next frame advanced, because every cue of
`specs/ui.md` is played from a frame.

### The run

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `startRun()` | Runs the game's run-start sequence of `specs/simulation.md`: every arm and wheel at its rest pose holding nothing, every wheel's six fixtures placed, the settle, `sim.cycle` at `0`, `sim.fraction` at `0`, `sim.speed` at `DEFAULT_SPEED_INDEX` (`1`), every set's tally at `0`, `sim.fault` `null`, and `sim.status` `running`. The readiness condition the `play` action applies is not applied, so a run starts on a machine with no rise and no set placed. |
| `stopRun()` | Returns `sim` to `null` and the machine to the editor, exactly as the run-stop sequence of `specs/simulation.md` does. |
| `setPaused(paused)` | Moves `sim.status` between `running` and `paused`, exactly as the `play` toggle moves it. It throws while the status is `faulted` or `complete`. |
| `setSpeed(index)` | Sets `sim.speed` to `index`, `0` to `3`. |
| `setCycle(n)` | Sets `sim.cycle` to `n`, a whole number of at least `0`, so the next cycle executes tape cell `n mod P` on every part, where `P` is the machine's period. |
| `setTally(index, n)` | Sets the tally of the open challenge's product `index` to `n`, a whole number of at least `0`. |
| `clearMotes()` | Removes every mote, fixtures included, and with them every filament and every grip. The area bank stands. |
| `spawnMote(q, r, type)` | Adds one unbonded, unheld mote of `type`, a name from `MOTES` in `specs/field.md`, resting on `(q, r)` with a fresh `id`. A hex off the field is posed like any other, as `specs/simulation.md` lets a mote rest off it. A hex already holding a mote throws. |
| `removeMote(mote)` | Removes that mote and every filament and grip touching it. A wheel's unwanted fixtures come off the field this way. |
| `linkMotes(a, b, weight)` | Joins motes `a` and `b` with one filament of `weight` `1` or `3`. Motes on hexes that are not adjacent, a fixture at either end, and a pair a filament already joins each throw. |
| `unlinkMotes(a, b)` | Removes the filament joining `a` and `b`. A pair no filament joins throws. |
| `setGrip(part, spoke, mote)` | That part's gripper on spoke direction `spoke`, `0` to `5`, takes hold of `mote`'s constellation. The spokes and the hexes are the part's as its live pose stands. A part with no gripper on that spoke, a mote resting anywhere but that gripper's hex, and a fixture each throw. |
| `releaseGrip(part, spoke)` | That gripper opens, leaving what it held resting where it stands. |
| `setPoseRotation(part, rotation)` | Sets that part's live rotation in `sim.poses`, `0` to `5`, leaving its rest rotation in `editor.parts` as it stands. |
| `setPoseLength(part, length)` | Sets that part's live length, `ARM_MIN_LEN` (`1`) to `ARM_MAX_LEN` (`3`), the same way. |
| `setPoseCell(part, q, r)` | Sets that part's live base cell, which is a cell of the track it is mounted on, the same way. |

`startRun` throws with no challenge open. Every other operation of this
group requires a live run and throws an `Error` without one. A new mote's
`id` is the `id` of the last entry of `sim.motes` in the next snapshot.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
A typical scenario calls `reset()`, `loadChallenge` to pose a small
challenge, `clearMachine()`, `setCompletion(false)`, `setAutoStep(false)`,
`startRun()`, and `clearMotes()`, places the one part and spawns the motes
it is about, advances the clock with `advance`, and reads the result from
`snapshot()`.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
A typical scenario runs the same sequence through the engine's `apply`, as
`engine.apply((s) => debug.spawnMote(s, 0, 0, "dust"))`: `reset`,
`loadChallenge` to pose a small challenge, `clearMachine`,
`setCompletion(false)`, `startRun`, and `clearMotes`, then the one part and
the motes it is about, then `engine.advance` over a clock of its own, and
reads the result from `debug.snapshot(engine.state)`.
{{else}}
A typical scenario calls `reset()`, `loadChallenge` to pose a small
challenge, `clearMachine()`, `setCompletion(false)`, `startRun()`, and
`clearMotes()`, places the one part and spawns the motes it is about,
advances the game with `engine.advance` over a clock of its own, and reads
the result from `snapshot()`.
{{/if}}

## Snapshot shape

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`snapshot` returns:
{{else}}
`snapshot()` returns:
{{/if}}

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "select" | "editor",
  mode: "campaign" | "extras",
  menuIndex: <number>,
  selectIndex: <number>,
  howtoPage: <number>,
  campaign: {
    count: <number>,           // how many challenges the shipped course holds
    unlockedCount: <number>,
    solved: [<number>],        // ascending indices
    records: [{ cost, cycles, area } | null],  // one entry per challenge
    stashed: [<number>],       // ascending indices with a stashed machine
    last: <number>,            // where the select screen lands
  },
  extras: {
    count: 10,
    solved: [<number>],
    records: [{ cost, cycles, area } | null],
    stashed: [<number>],
    last: <number>,
  },
  challenge: {                 // the challenge open in the editor
    name: <string>,
    reagents: [<molecule>],    // exactly the formats of specs/formats.md
    products: [<molecule>],
    permitted: [<part kind>],
    target: <number>,
    source: "campaign" | "extras" | "custom",
    index: <number | null>,    // null when source is "custom"
  } | null,
  editor: {
    parts: [                   // placement order; the tape panel's row order
      { id, kind, q, r, rotation, length,
        cells: [{ q, r }] | null, closed: <boolean | null>,
        index: <number | null>, tape: [<instruction | null>] | null },
    ],
    cost: <number>,
    period: <number>,
    selected: <number | null>,
    focus: "field" | "tape",
    cursor: { part: <number>, col: <number> } | null,
    drag: {                  // the live drag, in one of its three shapes
      kind: "place",
      part: <part kind>, index: <number | null>,
      rotation: <number>, length: <number>,
      at: { q, r } | null,   // the targeted hex, null off every hex
    } | {
      kind: "move",
      part: <part id>,
      from: { q, r },        // the hex the press grabbed
      at: { q, r } | null,
    } | {
      kind: "lay",
      part: <part id>,
      end: "first" | "last",
    } | null,
    undoDepth: <number>,
    redoDepth: <number>,
  },
  sim: {
    status: "running" | "paused" | "faulted" | "complete",
    cycle: <number>,
    fraction: <number>,
    speed: <number>,           // the SPEEDS index
    motes: [
      { id, q, r,              // the hex at the last boundary
        x: <number>, y: <number>,  // the drawn position at the current fraction
        type: <mote type>,
        wheel: <number | null> },
    ],
    filaments: [{ a: <mote id>, b: <mote id>, weight: 1 | 3 }],
    poses: [{ part, rotation, length, cell: { q, r } }],
    grips: [{ part, spoke, mote }],
    tallies: [<number>],       // one entry per product
    area: <number>,            // distinct hexes banked so far
    fault: { kind, parts: [<part id>], motes: [<mote id>] } | null,
    metrics: { cost, cycles, area } | null,
  } | null,
  pointer: { x: <number>, y: <number>, down: <boolean> },
  completion: <boolean>,       // the completion switch
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
  autoStep: <boolean>,         // whether the frame loop advances the simulation
{{/if}}
  muted: <boolean>,
  simTime: <number>,           // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
}
```

The shape is fixed, and every field is present whatever the screen and mode.
A field the current situation does not use reports its resting value rather
than going missing:

| Field | Resting value |
| --- | --- |
| `challenge` | `null` away from the editor |
| `editor` | present always; empty `parts`, `cost` `0`, `period` `1`, `selected` and `cursor` `null`, `focus` `"field"`, `drag` `null`, both depths `0` when nothing is open |
| `sim` | `null` while editing |
| `campaign.unlockedCount` | `1` |
| `campaign.stashed`, `extras.stashed` | empty |
| `campaign.last`, `extras.last`, `selectIndex` | `0` |
| `howtoPage` | `0` away from `howto` |
| `completion` | `true` |
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
| `autoStep` | `true` |
{{/if}}

Every field is read straight off the game's state, so what the snapshot
reports is what the game holds. The derived ones are:

| Field | Derived from |
| --- | --- |
| `editor.cost` | `PART_COSTS` over the parts, as `specs/parts.md` computes it |
| `editor.period` | the tapes, as `specs/instructions.md` computes it |
| a mote's `x`, `y` | its hex, its motion, and `sim.fraction`, by the formulas of `specs/field.md` and `specs/simulation.md` |
| `sim.area` | the length of the area bank |
| `campaign.count`, `extras.count` | the length of each mode's challenge list |
| `campaign.stashed`, `extras.stashed` | the challenges each mode holds a stashed machine for |

Three fields need the game to keep them honest every frame: `pointer`
mirrors the position and press the pointer input reports, `muted` mirrors
the runtime's mute bit, and `simTime` accumulates the delta time of every
update, whatever the screen.

## Diagnostics

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The runtime's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources.
{{else}}
The engine's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources.
{{/if}}
Register at least the current `screen` and `mode`, the open challenge's name
and source, the part count and `cost`, the `period`, the run's `status`,
`cycle`, `fraction`, and `speed` when one is active, each product's tally
against the `target`, the mote count, the banked `area`, the fault kind when
there is one, the focus, and the pointer position, the same facts the
snapshot reports. Keep each one short enough to read on a line, and keep
every source a pure read, so watching the overlay leaves the game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`) shows and hides
it, it is off when the game starts, and it reads the game without changing
it. It is a diagnostic layer rather than part of the game's presentation, so
keep it visually plain and clearly separate from the editor's own panels.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Orrery's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the
read, as `DeepReadonly<OrreryState>`, and reads off that argument: a source
that closed over the state `initialize` built would report the title screen
forever, because every frame leaves a new value behind. Drawing the panel,
toggling it with the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`), and
keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Orrery's part, through
`world.diagnostics` in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a
function of no arguments, called at each read; read the live state at the
call, through the world the mode holds, so the panel reports the frame being
drawn. Drawing the panel, toggling it with the backtick key
(`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`), and keeping it read-only are the
engine's.
{{/if}}
